\title{Closed Intervals in the Real Line Are Compact}
\taxon{Theorem}
\import{macros}
\p{
    For real numbers #{a\leq b}, the closed interval
    #{[a,b]\subseteq\mathbb{R}} is compact in the Euclidean topology.
}
\proof{
    The case #{a=b} is immediate. Assume #{a<b}, and let #{\mathcal{U}} be an
    open cover of #{[a,b]}. Define
    ##{
        S=\{x\in[a,b]\mid [a,x]\text{ is covered by finitely many members of }
        \mathcal{U}\}.
    }
    A member of the cover containing #{a} also contains a short interval to
    the right of #{a}, so #{S} is nonempty. It is bounded above by #{b}; let
    #{c=\sup S}, using the [least-upper-bound property](thm-0003).

    Suppose #{c<b}. Choose #{U\in\mathcal{U}} containing #{c}. Since #{U} is
    open in the subspace #{[a,b]}, some #{\varepsilon>0} satisfies
    ##{
        (c-\varepsilon,c+\varepsilon)\cap[a,b]\subseteq U.
    }
    Choose #{x\in S} with #{c-\frac{\varepsilon}{2}<x\leq c}. A finite cover of
    #{[a,x]}, together with #{U}, covers #{[a,y]} for some #{y>c}. This places
    #{y} in #{S}, contradicting that #{c} is an upper bound. Hence #{c=b}.

    Finally choose a member of the cover containing #{b}. For some
    #{\delta>0}, it contains every #{x\in[a,b]} satisfying
    #{b-\delta<x\leq b}. Since #{b=\sup S}, choose
    #{x\in S} with #{b-\delta<x\leq b}. A finite cover of #{[a,x]} together
    with this last member covers all of #{[a,b]}. Therefore every open cover
    has a finite subcover.
}
